<objective>
The developer's words: *"Quand je fais un changement de paramètre (comme la piste) je ne
vois pas le bouton enregistrer apparaître"* — which escalated, across two rounds of
follow-up, into the most severe item in this phase.

**What is CONFIRMED.** On a real iPhone in Safari and then on a Mac in desktop Safari
with a real mouse, **no setting on any settings page saves via auto-save**, with no error
shown and no way to retry. Phase 27 removed the manual save button. If nothing saves in
Safari, that is a live regression for any Safari user — very plausibly including the
developer's own primary device.

**What is NOT confirmed, and must not be claimed.** A dedicated deep-dive read
`dirty-state.js` end to end, the toast element and its CSS, script load order, and every
fetch/CSP option in use. It found **no** WebKit-incompatible API, **no** syntax error and
**no** obvious CSS collision. `requestIdleCallback`, `keepalive`, top-level `await`,
shadow DOM, `FormData` and `sendBeacon` were each checked and each ruled out — most are
absent from the codebase entirely. The one genuinely-real separate defect found along the
way (the leave-guard relying solely on `beforeunload`, which iOS Safari does not reliably
fire) is **not** the cause of "nothing saves," because there is no unload-time flush for
it to fail. A second close reading turned up nothing further.

**The exact root cause could not be confirmed without a live, tethered WebKit debugger,
which this environment does not have.**

**So this plan does not guess.** The decision taken with the developer on 2026-09-15 is
to build resilience: make a failure impossible to miss, and make it recoverable. That is
a smaller claim than "fixed," and it is the only claim the evidence supports.

**THE OVERCLAIM BAN IS A HARD GATE ON THIS PLAN.** Do not write an acceptance criterion,
a check description, a comment, a commit message or a SUMMARY sentence asserting that
auto-save now works in Safari. Nobody here can verify that without a reproduction and a
debugger. What this plan proves is: *a failure is always visible and always recoverable.*

**One door stays open.** CONTEXT.md's clause (d): if live Chromium testing while building
this surfaces a genuine, reproducible defect in the save pipeline, fix it and name it
plainly as a real root-cause fix. Do not fold a real find into resilience framing — and
equally, do not manufacture one to have something to report.
</objective>

<decisions_covered>
CONTEXT.md "Save reliability — escalated, most severe item in this phase (CFG-74)",
requirements (1), (2), (3) and (5). Requirement (4) — the runway `form=` regression-proof
check — is deliberately 28-07's, so that this plan's diff stays additive and reviewable
and the regression check is written against the finished behaviour rather than alongside
it.
</decisions_covered>

