  <action>
    **Pin the status.** Make `.save-status` survive scrolling. Prefer `position: sticky`
    with a `top` offset over `position: fixed`: sticky keeps the element in normal flow,
    so it reserves its own space and cannot overlap content the way the retired save bar
    did — which is precisely CFG-31's recorded defect. Use `fixed` only if sticky is
    provably unworkable in this layout (a scrolling ancestor), and if so say exactly why
    in the rule's comment and add whatever clearance the layout then needs.

    Respect the existing z-index ladder: tab bar 20, toast 40. The status belongs BELOW
    the toast (a failure message must be able to cover a status word, never the reverse)
    and must not collide with the tab bar at phone width. State the chosen value and its
    place in the ladder in the rule's comment — the file's convention is that the ladder
    is argued, not assumed.

    **This is not a banner and not a drawer.** Do not add a full-bleed background, do not
    span the viewport, do not re-create the dirty save bar's docked card. It is one quiet
    line of `.text-label` that stays reachable.

    **Supersede all four comment blocks in writing.** Each of them states, as a
    conclusion, that `.save-status` is not fixed / sits in normal flow / needs no z-index
    / needs no content-clearance rule. This plan makes each of those false. For EACH
    block: keep its original sentences, mark them SUPERSEDED by 28-06/CFG-74, and add the
    new fact plus the reason (a save status the visitor has scrolled past is a save
    status that cannot report a failure — which is the whole point of this requirement).
    Deleting any of them would recreate exactly the hole 27-03 and 27-04 refused to leave.

    **The retry affordance.** Render it in `_save_status_region_html()` (or immediately
    beside it, inside the same region) as a real `<button type="button">` carrying its own
    data attribute hook and its translated label. Add the English source string beside
    `SAVE_STATUS_SAVING_TEXT`/`SAVE_STATUS_SAVED_TEXT` and its French entry
    (`"Réessayer"`) to `companion/i18n_fr/display.py`.

    It must be **completely absent from the visitor's view at rest** — the developer's
    "zero boutons" stance is only survivable here because this control is conditional and
    load-bearing. Hide it by default in CSS and reveal it by a state class/attribute the
    script sets on failure. Follow `.quick-toast`'s own precedent for WHY it is hidden the
    way it is: the toast is kept in the accessibility tree with `opacity: 0` +
    `pointer-events: none` rather than `display: none`, because a live region added to the
    tree at announce time is one screen readers routinely miss. Decide deliberately which
    treatment the retry control needs — a `<button>` that is invisible but focusable is a
    tab-stop to nowhere, which is worse than a missed announcement — and write the
    decision and its reason into the rule's comment either way.

    **Do not** give it the accent fill: the accent-reservation list in `style.css`'s
    header comment is kept exhaustive on purpose, and a conditional recovery control is
    not on it. If the design genuinely needs a new accent consumer, extend that list in
    the header comment first, in the same edit, with the argument.

    Keep the region EMPTY at rest and keep `role="status"` / `aria-live="polite"` — the
    two existing `test_config_page.py` checks that pin this are correct and this task must
    not weaken them. If either needs updating for the new markup, update the assertion,
    never the contract.

    **While editing this stylesheet, there is a standing two-phase-old debt** recorded in
