<threat_model>
## Trust Boundaries

| Boundary | Description |
|----------|-------------|
| browser script → POST /settings | the auto-save posts the whole settings form on the session-gated route; a retry re-posts it |
| server → browser | the 204/303/200 negotiation, plus an opaque redirect from an expired session |
| script → diagnostic sink | a new failure-kind diagnostic is written to a log line and/or a `data-*` attribute |

## STRIDE Threat Register

| Threat ID | Category | Component | Disposition | Mitigation Plan |
|-----------|----------|-----------|-------------|-----------------|
| T-28-06-A | Information Disclosure | a failure-kind diagnostic leaking a field value into a log or the DOM | mitigate | the diagnostic records only the KIND and, for a non-204, the status code; the acceptance criteria require reading every new diagnostic site to confirm no user data reaches it |
| T-28-06-B | Denial of Service | a retry control that can be clicked repeatedly, or a timeout that leaves timers accumulating | mitigate | the retry routes through the existing `saving`/`pendingSave` serialisation (one request in flight, one coalesced follow-up); the timer is cleared on both settle paths and handlers are attached once |
| T-28-06-C | Tampering | a late response from a timed-out save overwriting a newer state | mitigate | the timed-out request's settle is made a no-op by construction, and the acceptance criteria require reading the guard |
| T-28-06-D | Spoofing | a status region claiming "Sauvegardé" over a save that did not land | mitigate | every failure kind clears the region and reveals the retry; Check B clause (ii) asserts it for all four kinds |
| T-28-06-E | Repudiation | claiming a Safari fix that has not been verified | mitigate | the overclaim ban is an acceptance criterion backed by ONE shared command (character-identical in 28-07) covering `companion/` BROADLY — source, check names and registered check descriptions across all three harnesses — plus REQUIREMENTS.md, ROADMAP.md and every Phase 28 artifact. It is enforced as a baseline-and-new-hits inventory rather than a zero-assertion, because the ban's own wording matches the pattern; every new hit is read and its verdict recorded |
| T-28-06-F | Tampering | a pinned status overlapping the tab bar or the toast, hiding real content | mitigate | sticky preferred over fixed so the element reserves its own space; the z-index choice is argued against the existing ladder; Check A asserts non-occlusion via `elementFromPoint` |
| T-28-06-SC | Tampering | npm/pip/cargo installs | n/a | zero packages installed; no build step, no bundler, no dependency, no new script file, no new route |
</threat_model>

